<SEOArticle title="10-Minute Desk Mobility Routine for Work" description="Follow a simple 10-minute desk mobility routine for the neck, shoulders, upper back, wrists, hips and ankles, plus a two-minute workday version." canonical="/workplace-wellness/desk-mobility-routine/" eyebrow="Movement at Work" heading="A 10-minute desk mobility routine for a better workday reset." lead="You do not need to correct every posture. Change position, move comfortable joints and return to work feeling more alert—not exhausted." {faqs} {toc} secondaryLabel="Ergonomics Checklist" secondaryHref="/workplace-wellness/workplace-ergonomics/">
 <p>Move slowly and use a comfortable range. This routine is general education, not treatment for pain or injury.</p>
 <h2 id="routine">The 10-minute desk mobility routine</h2>
 <ol>
  <li><strong>Stand and breathe — 60 seconds.</strong> Stand comfortably, look away from the screen and use slow breaths without forcing a specific count.</li>
  <li><strong>Walk — 90 seconds.</strong> Walk to water, down a hallway or around the available space.</li>
  <li><strong>Shoulder circles — 45 seconds.</strong> Move the shoulders forward and backward through an easy range.</li>
  <li><strong>Upper-back rotation — 60 seconds.</strong> Sit or stand tall and rotate gently from side to side without forcing the neck.</li>
  <li><strong>Reach and side bend — 60 seconds.</strong> Reach one arm overhead and create length through the side of the body; alternate sides.</li>
  <li><strong>Hands and wrists — 60 seconds.</strong> Open and close the hands, circle the wrists and vary finger positions.</li>
  <li><strong>Supported hip movement — 90 seconds.</strong> Hold a stable desk or chair and alternate a shallow squat, step-back or gentle hip hinge.</li>
  <li><strong>Calf raises and ankle movement — 60 seconds.</strong> Rise onto the toes with support, lower with control and circle each ankle.</li>
  <li><strong>Eye break — 45 seconds.</strong> Look toward a farther object and allow the eyes to change focus.</li>
  <li><strong>Reset the workstation — 90 seconds.</strong> Sit or stand, bring work close and make one useful adjustment.</li>
 </ol>
 <div class="callout"><strong>The routine should leave you ready to work.</strong> Reduce the range or skip any movement that causes sharp, radiating or worsening symptoms.</div>
 <h2 id="short">A two-minute version</h2>
 <ul><li>Stand and take several comfortable breaths.</li><li>Walk for one minute.</li><li>Circle the shoulders and open the hands.</li><li>Perform several supported calf raises.</li><li>Look away from the screen before returning.</li></ul>
 <h2 id="schedule">Build movement into the workday</h2>
 <p>Attach movement to transitions that already happen:</p>
 <ul><li>After a video meeting</li><li>Before starting a new project</li><li>When refilling water</li><li>After finishing a focused work block</li><li>Before and after lunch</li></ul>
 <p>Employees should not have to hide reasonable position changes. Employers can support movement by designing workloads and norms that make short breaks practical.</p>
 <h2>Desk mobility is not a substitute for ergonomics</h2>
 <p>A routine cannot compensate for equipment that cannot be adjusted, repeated high-force work or a culture that prevents breaks. Use the companion <a href="/workplace-wellness/workplace-ergonomics/">workplace ergonomics checklist</a> to review the workstation and employer process.</p>
 <h2 id="safety">When to stop and get help</h2>
 <p>Stop for sharp pain, new numbness, weakness, dizziness, loss of balance or symptoms that spread. Persistent or worsening concerns require appropriate professional evaluation.</p>
 <h2>Use this in a corporate wellness program</h2>
 <p>Employers can share the routine as one accessible option within a broader <a href="/corporate-wellness/">corporate wellness program</a>. Provide seated alternatives and avoid turning movement breaks into public performance contests.</p>
